{
 "id": "l12",
 "label": "12",
 "title": "Using may and mayroón",
 "summary": "Saying there is / there are and has / have with may and mayroón, and saying none with walâ",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Use may before a noun, adjective, number, mga or verb",
  "Use mayroón (or meron) before short words like ka, pa, ba and before pronouns",
  "Turn a may sentence into a mayroón sentence",
  "Answer a may or mayroón question with mayroón or walâ (not hindî)",
  "Use walâ to say there is no, does not have, or none"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "May and mayroón: there is, has",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Sentences with **may** or **mayroón** (said *may-ro-on*) point to the **existence** of things (*there is* or *there are*) or to **possession** (*has* or *have*)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Both words can mean *there is / there are* and *has / have*, but you **cannot swap one for the other freely**. Each has its own rules, shown below. The opposite of both is **walâ**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Meaning",
      "What comes right after it"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[may|pred]]",
       "there is / there are; has / have",
       "a noun, an adjective, a number, mga, or a verb"
      ],
      [
       "[[mayroón|pred]]  (short form [[meron|pred]])",
       "has / have",
       "a short word (ka, na, nang, pa, ba, din, daw) or a personal pronoun, with a ligature"
      ],
      [
       "[[walâ|pred]]",
       "there is / there are no; do / does not have; none",
       "a pronoun or short word (with -ng), or -ng and then a noun, adjective, number, mga or verb"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The three words at a glance"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "How to use may",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. May + a noun"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**May** is followed **immediately by a noun**. It works in both word orders: the subject-predicate order (with ay) and the inverted order. May sentences often do not say who the owner is (see the last sentence)."
    },
