  {
   "title": "How to use mayroón",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. Mayroón + a short word"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Mayroón** is followed by **one-syllable words** such as **ka, na, nang, pa, ba, din** and **daw** (Lesson Ten covers these words). The ligature is then attached to the short word that sits **nearest to the noun or adjective**. Mayroón may be shortened to **meron**."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Mayroón pang trabáho si Lucio.",
       "en": "Lucio still has a job.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mayroón",
         "has",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "pang",
         "still (pa + ligature)",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "trabáho",
         "job",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "(name marker)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Lucio",
         "Lucio",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Meron ka bang bágong aklát?",
       "en": "Do you have a new book?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Meron",
         "have",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ka",
         "you",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "bang",
         "(question word + ligature)",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "bágong",
         "new (+ ligature)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "aklát",
         "book",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Where does the -ng go?",
     "t": "In *Meron ka bang bágong aklát?* there are two short words, **ka** and **ba**. The ligature goes on **ba** (bang), because ba is the one nearest to the noun phrase."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "2. Mayroón + a personal pronoun"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In inverted sentences, mayroón is followed by a **personal pronoun** such as **akó, ka, tayó, kamí, kayó** and **silá** to show possession. These pronouns end in vowels, so the ligature **-ng** is added to them (kamí + ng = **kamíng**)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To change a **may** sentence into a **mayroón** sentence, rearrange **may + noun + pronoun** into **mayroón + pronoun + -ng + noun**."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "From may to mayroón",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "May",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "May",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "báhay",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "kamí",
         "r": "pron"
        },
        {
         "t": "sa Maynilà",
         "r": "other"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Mayroón",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Mayroón",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "kamí",
         "r": "pron"
        },
        {
         "t": "-ng",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "báhay",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "sa Maynilà",
         "r": "other"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "We have a house in Manila."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "May báhay kamí sa Maynilà.",
       "en": "We have a house in Manila.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "May",
         "have",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "báhay",
         "house",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "kamí",
         "we",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "sa",
         "in",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Maynilà",
         "Manila",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mayroón kamíng báhay sa Maynilà.",
       "en": "We have a house in Manila.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mayroón",
         "have",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "kamíng",
         "we (+ ligature)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "báhay",
         "house",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "sa",
         "in",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Maynilà",
         "Manila",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "3. Mayroón as an answer"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Mayroón** on its own answers a question. The word **oo** (*yes*) at the start, and the noun or pronoun after mayroón, are both **optional**."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "May bágong sapátos ba si Bob? (Oo), mayroón (siyá).",
       "en": "Does Bob have a new pair of shoes? (Yes), he has.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "May",
         "has",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "bágong",
         "new (+ ligature)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "sapátos",
         "shoes",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ba",
         "(question word)",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "(name marker)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Bob",
         "Bob",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "Oo",
         "yes",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "mayroón",
         "has",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "siyá",
         "he",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "The ma- prefix is a short may"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The **ma-** prefix that is added to root words to make other words is really a **shortened form of may**:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Word",
      "Comes from",
      "Literally"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[marámi|pred]] (many)",
       "may damí",
       "there are many"
      ],
      [
       "[[mataás|pred]] (tall)",
       "may taás",
       "there is height"
      ],
      [
       "[[malinís|pred]] (clean)",
       "may linís",
       "there is clean"
      ],
      [
       "[[mabutó|pred]] (bony)",
       "",
       "having many bones"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Ma- = may"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Ma- in short",
     "t": "With **abstract** nouns (ideas such as height), ma- means **there is / there are**. With **concrete** nouns (things you can touch, such as bones), ma- means **having many**: mabutó, bony."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "How to use walâ",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To deny that something exists or is owned, **replace may and mayroón with walâ**. Walâ turns *there is / there are* into **there is / there are no**, and *has / have* into **does not / do not have**. On its own, or as an answer to a mayroón question, walâ means **none**."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Walâ follows three simple rules, based on the may and mayroón rules."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. Walâ + a pronoun or short word"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Walâ usually comes at the **start of the sentence** and is followed by a **possessive pronoun or a one-syllable word**. The ligature **-ng** is added to the pronoun or short word that comes after it."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Walâ akóng kaibígan sa Quezon.",
       "en": "I do not have a friend in Quezon.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Walâ",
         "do not have",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "akóng",
         "I (+ ligature)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "kaibígan",
         "friend",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "sa",
         "in",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Quezon",
         "Quezon",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Walâ siláng mga púnong-kahoy.",
       "en": "They do not have trees.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Walâ",
         "do not have",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "siláng",
         "they (+ ligature)",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "mga",
         "(plural)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "púnong-kahoy",
         "trees",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Walá pang bágong aklát.",
       "en": "There is no new book yet.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Walá",
         "there is no",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "pang",
         "yet (pa + ligature)",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "bágong",
         "new (+ ligature)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "aklát",
         "book",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Walá nang trabáho si Lucio.",
       "en": "Lucio does not have a job now (or anymore).",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Walá",
         "does not have",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "nang",
         "now / anymore",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "trabáho",
         "job",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "(name marker)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Lucio",
         "Lucio",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "2. Walâ + -ng before other words"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Walâ **with the ligature -ng** (walâng) is used before **numbers or adjectives, nouns, the article mga, and verbs**, just like may."
    },
    {
